@* A labelled row of boxes enumerating where an answer comes from.

   WHEN THIS IS ALLOWED
   ==================================================================================
   Only where the set of boxes is LITERAL: a fixed, short, enumerable set the page has to show
   anyway. The five card positions in a pack. The twenty wishlist slots. The four routes to a
   card. The five cards in an offer.

   Not a general-purpose chart, and specifically not a way to draw a ranking. A ranking of 139
   parallel foils has no fixed set to enumerate, and a strip of the top five would be a chart that
   implies the other 134 are not there. Those pages get a verdict, a sentence and a list.

   The test is whether you could name every box before looking at the data. If you could not, this
   is the wrong component and the page wants a list.

   WHY A STRIP RATHER THAN A TABLE
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Because a box can be DASHED, and a table cell cannot. "This route does not exist for this
   rarity" and "this position can only ever give you a duplicate" are facts, and a blank cell
   reports them as data that failed to arrive. A dashed box with an em dash in it says the thing
   that is true.

   It also reads as a shape. Seventeen solid boxes and three dashed ones at the end says "nearly
   right" without anyone counting to twenty, which is the whole job on the wishlist page. *@

<div class="box-strip">
    @if (Label is { Length: > 0 })
    {
        <span class="micro">
            @Label
            @* One clause for the whole strip rather than a note per box: what makes the dead boxes
               dead is almost always one fact about the pack or the rarity, and saying it five
               times is five chances to read it as five different facts. *@
            @if (Clause is { Length: > 0 })
            {
                <span class="clause">@Clause</span>
            }
        </span>
    }

    @* The column count is the page's, because it is a property of the set being enumerated: five
       for a pack, four for the routes to a card, ten for a board of twenty in two rows. Inline
       because it is data rather than a design token -- there is no stylesheet rule that could know
       how many cards a pack holds. *@
    <div class="boxes" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(@Columns,minmax(0,1fr))">
        @ChildContent
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The micro-label above the row. Omitted where the strip follows its own heading.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Label { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Why the dead boxes are dead, in a few words. Left null where none of them are, so the
    /// strip does not carry a sentence explaining an absence that is not there.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Clause { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Boxes across. One row unless the page says otherwise — twenty slots go ten across in two
    /// rows, because twenty in a line is a line nobody reads the shape of.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int Columns { get; set; } = 5;

    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
